# Solution — GIAM 5.3 Problem 12

## Problem

Give an inductive proof that $ \forall n\ge3 \in\mathbb{N} $, $ n^{3}+3 > n^{2}+3n+1 $.

## Answer

$$$
\boxed{\;D(k+1) = D(k) + 3k^{2}+k-3, \quad D(n) = \left(n^{3}+3\right)-\left(n^{2}+3n+1\right)\;}
$$$

The gap starts positive at the basis, $ D(3) = 30-19 = 11 $, and the increment $ 3k^{2}+k-3 $ is positive for every $ k\ge1 $. Verified for every $ n $ from $ 3 $ to $ 5000 $.

## The Proof

Write $ D(n) = \left(n^{3}+3\right)-\left(n^{2}+3n+1\right) = n^{3}-n^{2}-3n+2 $. The claim is that $ D(n) > 0 $ for $ n\ge3 $.

> **Theorem.** For every natural number $ n\ge3 $, $ n^{3}+3 > n^{2}+3n+1 $.
>
> **Proof.** By induction on $ n $, starting at $ 3 $.
>
> **Basis.** At $ n=3 $, $ 3^{3}+3 = 30 $ and $ 3^{2}+9+1 = 19 $, and $ 30 > 19 $.
>
> **Inductive step.** Let $ k\ge3 $ be a particular but arbitrarily chosen natural number and suppose $ D(k) > 0 $. Expanding,
>
> $ \begin{aligned} D(k+1) &= (k+1)^{3}-(k+1)^{2}-3(k+1)+2 \\ &= \left(k^{3}+3k^{2}+3k+1\right)-\left(k^{2}+2k+1\right)-3k-3+2 \\ &= \left(k^{3}-k^{2}-3k+2\right)+3k^{2}+k-3 \\ &= D(k)+3k^{2}+k-3 . \end{aligned} $
>
> Since $ k\ge1 $, $ 3k^{2}+k-3 \ge 3+1-3 = 1 > 0 $. Hence $ D(k+1) > D(k) > 0 $, which says $ (k+1)^{3}+3 > (k+1)^{2}+3(k+1)+1 $.
>
> Basis and step together give the result for every $ n\ge3 $. $ \blacksquare $

As in Problems 10 and 11, the step uses less than it is given: $ k\ge1 $ suffices. The basis is what pins the theorem to $ n\ge3 $.

## The Picture

The gap factors, and its sign pattern explains every small case.

!(78%)[The two sides, their gap, and the factored cubic](problem-12-gap.svg "A diagram in four parts. The top band gives the step identity D of k plus one equals D of k plus three k squared plus k minus three, with D of 3 equal to 11. The left panel tabulates both sides and their gap for n from zero to seven: the inequality holds at n equal to zero, fails at one, is an equality at two, and holds from three on. The right panel graphs D of x, the product of x minus two and x squared plus x minus one, with roots at minus the golden ratio, its reciprocal, and two, and the integer points zero to three marked. A green band gives the factorisation, and an orange band notes that the step works from k equal to one while the basis must be three.")

## Remark — The Gap Factors

Since $ D(2) = 8-4-6+2 = 0 $, the number $ 2 $ is a root, and dividing out $ n-2 $ gives

$$$
D(n) = n^{3}-n^{2}-3n+2 = (n-2)\left(n^{2}+n-1\right) ,
$$$

checked for $ \lvert n\rvert $ up to $ 1000 $. For $ n\ge3 $ both factors are positive, which proves the theorem without induction. It also explains the small cases: the factor $ n-2 $ gives equality at $ n=2 $; at $ n=1 $ the factors are $ -1 $ and $ 1 $, so the inequality fails; at $ n=0 $ both are negative, so it holds.

The quadratic factor has roots $ \frac{-1\pm\sqrt5}{2} $, that is, $ 1/\varphi\approx0.618 $ and $ -\varphi\approx-1.618 $, where $ \varphi = \frac{1+\sqrt5}{2} $ is the golden ratio. So over the reals the inequality holds exactly on $ (-\varphi, 1/\varphi) \cup (2,\infty) $. The golden ratio appears because $ x^{2}+x-1 = 0 $ is its defining equation in disguise: substituting $ x = 1/y $ gives $ y^{2} = y+1 $.

## Remark — The Basis, and a Weaker Starting Point

The step is valid from $ k=1 $, yet neither $ n=1 $ (where $ D=-1 $) nor $ n=2 $ (where $ D=0 $) can serve as the basis of the strict inequality. But the step adds at least $ 1 $, so it upgrades a weak inequality to a strict one: from $ D(2)\ge0 $ it gives $ D(3) \ge 0+3\cdot4+2-3 = 11 > 0 $. So an induction for $ D(n)\ge0 $ from the basis $ n=2 $ yields the strict result for $ n\ge3 $.

## Remark — Problems 10 to 12 Side by Side

The last three problems share one pattern: a polynomial or exponential inequality that fails for small $ n $, a basis at the first $ n $ where it holds, and a step that compares how fast the two sides grow. In each the step works earlier than the basis does, so the basis decides where the theorem begins.

$$$
\begin{array}{l|c|c|c}
\text{problem} & \text{fails at} & \text{basis} & \text{step valid for} \\ \hline
10:\ 3n^{2}+3n+1 < 2n^{3} & 0,1,2 & 3 & k\ge1 \\
11:\ n^{3} < 3^{n} & 3 & 4 & k\ge3 \\
12:\ n^{3}+3 > n^{2}+3n+1 & 1,2 & 3 & k\ge1
\end{array}
$$$
